{"id":"premium-advanced","title":"Advanced SAT Math","description":"Complex algebra, calculus concepts, and advanced geometry","difficulty":"advanced","category":"math","tags":["calculus","advanced-algebra","complex-geometry"],"questions":[{"id":"ADV-001","type":"multiple_choice","content":"The derivative of f(x) = x³ - 6x² + 9x - 2 at x = 2 is:","options":[{"id":"A","text":"-3"},{"id":"B","text":"0"},{"id":"C","text":"3"},{"id":"D","text":"6"}],"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"f'(x) = 3x² - 12x + 9. At x = 2: f'(2) = 3(4) - 12(2) + 9 = 12 - 24 + 9 = -3.","difficulty":"advanced","topics":["calculus","derivatives"],"wordCount":17,"estimatedTime":30,"contentHash":"1854ec16b91d07365bf03dbdd329850e"},{"id":"ADV-002","type":"multiple_choice","content":"In a regular pentagon, what is the measure of each interior angle?","options":[{"id":"A","text":"108°"},{"id":"B","text":"120°"},{"id":"C","text":"135°"},{"id":"D","text":"144°"}],"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"For a regular n-gon, each interior angle = (n-2)×180°/n. For pentagon: (5-2)×180°/5 = 540°/5 = 108°.","difficulty":"advanced","topics":["geometry","polygons","angles"],"wordCount":12,"estimatedTime":30,"contentHash":"cd710d0d9114a8d3d5df06180d7a9e42"}],"version":"1.0.0","generatedAt":"2025-11-21T23:46:44.542Z","wordCount":29,"estimatedTime":60}